# Provenance of payload/frpc-mipsel-v3

This is the frpc the device runs. It is **not** the asset published by
`neroxps/frp-v3` releases, because that one cannot talk to the frp v3 server it
belongs to - see "why" below. It is built here, from public upstream source plus
the fork's two documented constants.

    upstream   fatedier/frp v0.71.0 (tag v0.71.0, codeload tarball)
    patch      neroxps/frp-v3 v3.0.0 patches/frp-v3.patch, both hunks:
                 pkg/util/net/websocket.go   FrpWebsocketPath  "/~!frp" -> "/api/v1/stream"
                 pkg/proto/wire/wire.go      MagicV2           "FRP\x00\x02\r\n" -> nine zero bytes
    toolchain  go1.27.1 windows/amd64, CGO_ENABLED=0
    build      GOOS=linux GOARCH=mipsle GOMIPS=softfloat
               -buildvcs=false -tags "frpc,noweb" -trimpath
               -ldflags "-s -w -X github.com/fatedier/frp/pkg/util/version.version=0.71.0-v3-patched"
    sha256     fb4852dff74e4d28893175a6bfc0b5829193114e2dd4699d4e85ab419a62c3f6
    size       17498303 bytes

## Why not the release asset

The fork's `src/` tree - the one its CI compiles and its releases are built from
- still contains both upstream constants (checked through the API on 2026-09-12),
and the released mipsle and amd64 assets confirm it (they contain `/~!frp` and no
`/api/v1/stream`). The server side does not work that way: the deployed frps on
the OMV *is* patched (a handshake to `10.89.1.3:7000/api/v1/stream` returns
`101 Switching Protocols`, while `/~!frp` gets no websocket answer at all), and
the Caddy front on 10443 routes `/api/v1/stream` only.

So with the released client the device sends `/~!frp`, the front answers 404 and
frpc reports `connect to server error: bad status`. Captured on the device by
pointing frpc's plaintext websocket login at a listener: the request line is
literally `GET /~!frp HTTP/1.1`.

This build closes that gap. Verified against the real server: over
`wss://home.neroxps.cn:10443` the login now reaches frps and is only rejected
with `token in login doesn't match token from configuration` (the router carries
the token of a different frps), i.e. transport, path, TLS, SNI and websocket
upgrade are all correct.

## How to replace it

Preferred: apply `patches/frp-v3.patch` inside the fork's `src/`, re-release, and

    tools/fetch-frpc-v3.ps1 -Tag <new tag>

which verifies the asset and refuses an unpatched one. Or rebuild by hand with
the flags above; `tools/scan-frpc-hardening.py` reports what a binary carries and
`--require-patched` turns a missing hunk into a hard failure:

    python3 tools/scan-frpc-hardening.py payload/frpc-mipsel-v3 --require-patched
